1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,500
In Chennai, everyone shares flood photos. Nobody can tell how deep the water is.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:23,500
People post photos during a flood, but a photo can't tell a driver or a rescue team how deep the water is. Vellam turns the photo into a number.

3
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:35,000
A live map of reports (these are labelled demo data), with area alerts built from clusters of nearby reports.

4
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:53,000
Four jobs, checked against each other. A measurement from geometry, an independent judgement from Muse Spark, and trust checks around both.

5
00:00:54,200 --> 00:01:06,200
Every stage is shown: the water SAM found, the wheels, the geometry, then Muse Spark's check.

6
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:17,430
Both readings appear with their uncertainty, so you can see why it said what it said.

7
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:34,630
When geometry and Muse Spark disagree, the report goes to review. A wrong depth is worse than none.

8
00:01:36,930 --> 00:01:43,930
Not a flood? Rejected, with the reason.

9
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:56,760
Ask by voice, in Tamil: is this road flooded? Muse Voice Transcribe listens and Muse Spark answers from the live reports.

10
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:06,460
We tested the real pipeline on 100 public photos of flooded cars with known depth levels.

11
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:15,714
Muse Spark's depth ranks with the true level: rank correlation 0.89, and 99% within one level.

12
00:02:16,414 --> 00:02:33,714
The test found real problems. Geometry is weak on tight crops, and our flood check was losing real reports. We fixed the check, and we say what it costs.

13
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:47,414
And in the real world: we measured waterlogged streets in Chennai with a tape and compared.

14
00:02:48,414 --> 00:02:57,414
Open source. Built for the people who have to drive through it.

